# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  client/.env.production
#
#  Loaded by Vite ONLY when building for production
#  (mode=production, i.e. `npm run build`). It is never read by the
#  dev server, which uses mode=development.
#
#  Safe to commit: every VITE_ variable is inlined into the browser bundle
#  and is therefore public by design. Never put a secret in a VITE_ var.
#
#  WHAT THIS FILE IS AND IS NOT
#  ---------------------------
#  It documents and pins the intended production configuration, but it does
#  NOT guarantee it. Verified on this repo: an environment variable set at
#  build time OVERRIDES the value here — `VITE_API_URL` set in the shell/hPanel
#  environment still reached the bundle even with the empty value below.
#  So the real control is: leave VITE_API_URL UNSET in the Hostinger
#  environment. This file then makes the fallback explicit rather than
#  accidental.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── API base ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Monolithic deployment: the Express server serves both the built client and
# the API from the SAME origin, so this MUST be empty. The client then uses
# relative /api, /uploads and /socket.io paths against the current origin.
#
# Set an absolute origin ONLY if the frontend and API are on different
# domains (a split deployment), and include the /api suffix, e.g.
# VITE_API_URL=https://api.example.com/api
#
# Do not define VITE_API_URL anywhere in the Hostinger application
# environment unless you are deliberately running a split deployment —
# a stray value silently repoints every API call.
VITE_API_URL=

# ── Feature flags ────────────────────────────────────────
# Evaluated as `=== "true"`, so any other value — including an unset
# variable — reads as false.
#
# HEADS UP: FEATURES in src/constants/index.js is exported but consumed by
# NO module (verified: its only references are its own definition and
# re-export). These flags have NO runtime effect today. They are declared
# here so the values are correct and consistent the day something reads
# them — not because they change behaviour now.
VITE_ENABLE_BARCODE_SCANNER=true
VITE_ENABLE_BOOK_LOOKUP=true
VITE_ENABLE_EDITIONS=true
VITE_ENABLE_DARK_MODE=true
VITE_ENABLE_ANIMATIONS=true
